  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1003 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nBy the start of the long term, a pattern change will be on our \ndoorsteps. Aloft, a long wave through will be approaching from the\nwest as ridging departs off the east coast. At the surface, this \nwill be accompanied by a surface cold front pushing across the \ncentral CONUS. By Thursday night into Friday we will find ourself \nsandwiched between high pressure to our east, and an approaching \ncold front to our west. This will place us solidly within \nsoutherly flow. This southerly flow will work as a pipeline for \nmoisture from the Gulf up into the TN Valley. At the surface, dew \npoints look to return to the 70s, while aloft, PWATs look to push \nwell above the 90th percentile for early October (2\"+). \n\nThis moisture will prime us for a very wet weekend. What remains\nsomewhat uncertain is how much forcing will present as the parent\ntrough looks to weaken through the weekend as it moves through the TN\nValley. This gives us some pause when considering weekend rain\nchances. The current question is if high rain chances will be\nprolonged over a wide spread area or if shower and storms will be\nmore scattered creating a more localized concern. Kept blended\nguidance for this forecast cycle with medium to high rain chances in\nplace Friday afternoon through Sunday afternoon. Flooding concerns\nwill be highly dependent on the location of prolonged rain and speed\nit falls. If the rain falls at a steady rate, local drainage may be\nable to handle it with minimal problems. If stronger storms develop,\nperiods of heavy rainfall could lead to some flood concerns. Please\ncheck back for updates.",
